# UI consistency — icons & shared features

## Rule

When a control or feature has the **same function and purpose** across menus/screens, its **icon and behavior must be identical** (deterministic). Do not invent a parallel variant per page.

Same purpose → same glyph, same interaction model, same confirm/feedback pattern, same shared component when one exists.

Different purpose → different icon/behavior is allowed (and preferred).

## Icons

- Action glyphs come from `src/lib/actionEmoji.ts` (`ActionEmoji`). Do not hardcode alternate emojis for edit/save/cancel/close/back/show/hide/drag/add/delete.
- Nav and domain icons (History, Summary, Settings, etc.) stay single-sourced where defined (e.g. `BottomNav` tab map); reuse that source if the same destination appears elsewhere.
- Do not swap icons for the same action for “variety” or screen-specific styling.

## List row actions

- Every **action control on a list row** (edit, delete, drag, show/hide, add-adjacent, etc.) must use the matching **emoji** from `ActionEmoji` — **never a text label** like "Edit", "Delete", "Save", or "Remove".
- Use `aria-label` / `title` for accessibility when the visible control is emoji-only; do not replace the emoji with visible text for the same purpose.
- Row content (name, amount, date, status) may still use text; this rule applies only to **action affordances** on the row.
- If a new row action has no `ActionEmoji` entry yet, add it there first, then use that constant everywhere.

## Behavior

- Reuse shared components for the same job: e.g. `ConfirmDialog` for destructive confirms, `SwipeDeleteRow` for swipe-to-delete, `CategoryPicker` / `CirclePicker` for pickers, toasts for non-blocking success.
- Same action → same steps: if delete confirms in one list, it confirms the same way in every equivalent list; if save shows a light toast in Quick Add, equivalent saves elsewhere do not use a blocking dialog unless purpose differs.
- Gestures, disabled states, and error recovery for a shared feature stay aligned across screens.

## When introducing or changing UI

1. Search for an existing control that already does this job; extend or reuse it.
2. If none exists and the action will appear in more than one place, extract a shared component or constant before duplicating.
3. If two screens look similar but the intent differs, use a distinct icon/label/flow so users are not misled.

## Examples

- ✅ Delete in History and category manage both use `ActionEmoji.delete` + the same confirm pattern.
- ✅ Edit affordance everywhere uses `ActionEmoji.edit`, not a mix of ✏️ / 🖊️ / “Edit” text-only.
- ✅ List row actions are emoji-only (`ActionEmoji.edit`, `ActionEmoji.delete`, …), with `aria-label` for screen readers — never a visible "Edit" / "Delete" button text on the row.
- ❌ A settings list row shows a text "Edit" link while History uses ✏️ for the same edit intent.
- ❌ History uses swipe-delete with confirm while Settings list deletes instantly with a different trash glyph for the same “remove this row” intent.
- ❌ One screen’s “close overlay” is ❌ and another’s identical close is ✕ or “X” text with different dismiss semantics.
